<h2>Age as the jurisdictional fact</h2>
<p>The POCSO Act protects children, and Section 2(1)(d) defines a child as any person below the age of eighteen years. Whether the Special Court framework, the presumptions, and the special procedures of the Act apply at all depends on the victim having been a child on the date of the alleged offence. Section 34(2) of the POCSO Act itself recognises this: if any question arises in any proceeding before the Special Court as to whether a person is a child, the Special Court shall determine the question after satisfying itself about the age of the person, and shall record its reasons in writing. Age is thus not a peripheral detail but the fact on which the jurisdiction of the entire statute rests, and it is routinely the most contested issue in a POCSO trial.</p>

<h2>The graded scheme of Section 94</h2>
<p>Section 94 of the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015 lays down the structured method of age determination. Under Section 94(1), where it is obvious from the appearance of the person that he or she is a child, the Committee or the Board may record an observation to that effect and proceed without waiting for further confirmation. Where, however, there are reasonable grounds for doubt, Section 94(2) requires the process of age determination to be undertaken by seeking evidence in a fixed order of preference. In practice, courts and investigating agencies apply this graded scheme as the structured method for establishing age in POCSO matters as well.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Step 1 — School or matriculation certificate</h4><p>The date of birth certificate from the school, or the matriculation or equivalent certificate from the concerned examination board, if available. This is the first and preferred category of proof.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Step 2 — Civil birth certificate</h4><p>In the absence of a Step 1 document, the birth certificate given by a corporation or a municipal authority or a panchayat.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Step 3 — Medical age determination, as last resort</h4><p>Only in the absence of both categories of documents, age is to be determined by an ossification test or any other latest medical age determination test, conducted on the orders of the Committee or the Board. The statute requires such a test to be completed within fifteen days from the date of the order.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The hierarchy is not a menu. Each rung is reached only in the absence of the rung above it, and Section 94(3) provides that the age recorded by the Committee or the Board shall, for the purposes of the Act, be deemed to be the true age of the person brought before it.</p>

<h2>Medical estimation and its inherent margin</h2>
<p>An ossification test assesses skeletal maturity, typically from radiographs of joints, and dental examination assesses the development of teeth. As a matter of medical science, such assessment estimates age within a range rather than fixing an exact date of birth, since individuals mature at different rates. The statutory text of Section 94 does not itself prescribe any arithmetical adjustment to a medical estimate; what it does is rank medical testing last, permit it only when documentary proof is absent, and require it to be completed within a fixed time. The inherent imprecision of medical estimation is best understood as the reason the legislature preferred documents, and it is a point on which the defence and the prosecution frequently lead expert evidence at trial.</p>
<div class="note"><p>Because the medical route is a last resort, the practical battle in most cases is over the documents: whether a school certificate is genuine, whether the date of birth recorded at first admission was supported by any underlying record, and whether a civil birth certificate relates to the person concerned. These are questions of proof under the ordinary law of evidence.</p></div>

<h2>Age of the accused: the claim of juvenility</h2>
<p>Age can matter on the other side of the case as well. If the person accused of a POCSO offence was himself below eighteen years on the date of the offence, he is dealt with under the Juvenile Justice Act rather than under the ordinary criminal process. Section 9(2) of the JJ Act, 2015 provides that if a person alleged to have committed an offence claims before a court, other than a Board, that he was a child on the date of the offence, the court shall make an inquiry, take such evidence as may be necessary (but not an affidavit) to determine the age, and record a finding. The statutory text is explicit that such a claim may be raised before any court and shall be recognised at any stage, even after final disposal of the case. If the claim is found correct, the court forwards the person to the Juvenile Justice Board, and the same Section 94 hierarchy governs how the age inquiry proceeds.</p>

<h2>Documents in Delhi practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <h4>Records commonly produced in Delhi courts on the question of age</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Date of birth certificate from the school first attended, with the admission register and admission form where available.</li>
    <li>Matriculation or equivalent certificate of the examination board concerned.</li>
    <li>Birth certificate issued by the Municipal Corporation of Delhi or the New Delhi Municipal Council, or by the local registrar of births and deaths.</li>
    <li>Where none of the above exists, the report of the medical board conducting the ossification or other age determination test, together with the underlying radiographs.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>Certified copies are ordinarily obtained through the investigating officer during investigation, and the authors or custodians of the records are cited as witnesses so that the documents are duly proved at trial.</p>

<h2>Closing observations</h2>
<p>Age determination under Section 94 of the JJ Act is a structured, document-first exercise, and in a POCSO case it decides both whether the Act applies to the victim and, where juvenility is claimed, which system deals with the accused. Since the outcome so often turns on the quality and proof of records, the question deserves early and careful attention from both sides; independent legal advice on specific facts is always advisable.</p>
